  "query_preview": "..\n(Wednesday night through next Monday)\nIssued at 120 PM MDT Tue Oct 6 2026\n\nBy Wednesday night into Thursday, the trough will be oriented east\nto west across southern NM. There could still be isolated showers\nand storms over the high terrain, but significant impacts are not\nexpected. The NAM has been showing significantly higher coverage \nand amounts of QPF for both Wed and Thu, but it is the outlier \nwith both precipitation as well as the upper air pattern, so it \nhas been largely ignored for this package. The trough may \nre-organize into a weak closed low as it continues to slide \nsoutheastward away from NM Friday into the weekend. Meanwhile, \nsouthwesterly flow will gradually increase, especially across the \nnorthwest half of the state through this time period and a lee\nside surface trough will develop across CO and NE NM. Thus, expect\nsome breezy conditions across northern NM (especially NE NM) Fri \nand Sat. \n\nAll eyes then turn to the potential for tropical moisture to\nreturn. Hurricane Rachel will get pushed northeastward Friday while\nit's still over the Pacific in response to a rather weak Pacific \nlow shifting southward. However, the Pacific low will eventually \nabsorb the remnants of Rachel on Saturday as Rachel is sheared \napart. As another more potent closed low digs southward over \nNorthern CA on Sunday, southwest flow ahead of this system will \nbring the moisture remnants of Hurricane Rachel and the original \nlow into SoCal and AZ Sunday and Sunday night. Some light \nprecipitation may reach as far east as the Rio Grande Valley \nSunday and Monday, but amounts look to be relatively light \noverall. However, that may change starting late Monday into \nTuesday. Though the remnants of Rachel will have shifted well to \nthe north of NM, another tropical system will have developed south\nof the Baja Peninsula. With the potent upper low still digging \nover the west coast Monday, the new tropical system should get \ndrawn northward. This system would also shear apart before \nreaching NM, but the moisture currently looks to take aim on the \nstate late Monday through Tuesday night. If this occurs, rain with\nisolated embedded thunderstorms would be on tap. It should be \nnoted that this is still one week out, and the specifics will \nchange. If flow aloft is more southwesterly vs. southerly, which \nboth the AI-GFS and AI-GEFS show, NM may not see as much \nprecipitation Monday night through Tuesday night. It's also \ncurious that ensembles keep the PWATs below 0.75\" through the \nperiod, which would suggest the moisture will be diverted. All \nthis to say that there is a lot of uncertainty early next week. \nStay tuned.",
